      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "DETECTION POST-PROCESSING: two sources -> one clean set\n\nThe numbered groups below are these five steps, in order; each group\nreads left to right and every wire that crosses a group boundary is\nnamed in the small note at that group's left edge.\n\nEvery detector in the install emits BOUNDING_BOX, which is a list of DICTS. The numbers inside it - the coordinates and the score/label keys - cannot be sorted, measured, merged or plotted while they stay dicts. 'CV BBoxes To Array' is the way out and 'CV Array To BBoxes' the way back; in between it is ordinary array work.\n\n1. TWO SOURCES. The same regions are found twice: tight boxes from the connected components, and looser boxes off a dilated copy of the mask. That is the situation anything real produces - two detectors, one detector at two thresholds, or one detector over a scale sweep - and it produces DUPLICATES.\n\n2. MERGE. Both sets become arrays, and 'CV Concat Arrays' appends them along axis 0 (rows). The same node merges their 'areas' arrays in lockstep, which is what keeps a per-box value aligned with its box.\n\n3. SUPPRESS. 'CV NMS Boxes' drops every box that overlaps a higher-scoring one by more than the IoU threshold. cv2's NMS was otherwise only reachable inside 'YOLO Detect Decode'. Here the score is the region AREA - wire a real confidence there when you have one. Wire 'class_ids' as well and suppression runs per class instead (that is the switch; there is no boolean widget).\n\n4. KEEP THE REST IN STEP. 'kept_indices' indexes the FLATTENED input order, so 'CV Take By Index' gathers any parallel array - here the areas - down to the survivors.\n\n5. MEASURE. 'CV Box IoU Matrix' is every-against-every overlap. Read the bright diagonal band: that is each tight box against its own padded twin, i.e. the duplicates NMS removed."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
